From 0c0609efdbab4549aef72b201b6947d6eca4e92e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 09:05:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] hs_cache: Add an off-by-default per-descriptor fetch
 rate limit

An HSDir queues the whole descriptor for every fetch it answers, and
nothing bounds the bandwidth it spends on any one descriptor. If a
flood such as the one in tor#41339 concentrates on a few descriptors,
an operator may want to cap that.

Give each cached descriptor a fetch token bucket, kept when the
descriptor is replaced, and add HSDirDescriptorFetchRate (default 5 per
second) and HSDirDescriptorFetchBurst (default 20). Fetches over the
limit are counted as tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_fetch_over_rate"},
which shows whether a flood concentrates on a few descriptors. When the
new HSDirDescriptorFetchLimit option is set, the HSDir also closes the
circuit of such a fetch with END_CIRC_REASON_RESOURCELIMIT before
queuing the descriptor, as HSDirOneRequestPerCircuit does, and counts it
as action "refused" in tor_relay_hsdir_get_total.

The bucket is shared by every client of a descriptor. While the limit
is enabled, anyone can use it up and deny that descriptor on this HSDir
until it refills (clients then try its other HSDirs, unless those
enable the limit too), and anyone can learn how often others fetch it
here by probing at the refill rate. That makes the limit a local,
emergency choice for saving bandwidth, not a network-wide defense, so
unlike the other HSDir defenses it has no consensus parameters and is
off by default. Popular services can also legitimately exceed the
default rate, which the counter shows.

Part of #41339.
